 [{"turn": 17, "text": "Opus 5.5's F7 dropped Black's lead from -7.1 to -17.8, a loss of 10.7 points. The engine preferred G2. It was the first big swing, and Black stayed far behind for the next two dozen moves."}, {"turn": 40, "text": "With Black at -31.2, the 14k's E9 lost 16.1 points and cut the deficit to -15.1. The engine's choice was D9. It gave Opus 5.5 a way back into a game that looked finished."}, {"turn": 49, "text": "Opus 5.5 played D4 from -4.0 and lost 22.1 points, falling to -26.1, while the engine wanted B5. The 14k answered A4, which lost 22.2 points of its own and put the lead back at -3.9. The two mistakes almost cancelled out."}, {"turn": 52, "text": "The 14k's B4 lost 6.3 points and turned a -3.8 deficit into a +2.5 lead for Black. It was the only time Black was ahead, and the lead held through the next few moves."}, {"turn": 61, "text": "Opus 5.5 played C1 where the engine wanted B1, losing 2.1 points and going from 0.0 to -2.1. White took B1 next. Every later move was about even in the analysis, so these 2.1 points decided the game."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py places one named move and reports captures and group liberties, libs.py prints group liberties, and count.py scores a given board with named dead stones; none of them loops over empty points to choose a move or plays out continuations, so they only inspect the current position.", "stop": false}}